WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:06.750
Millions of people around the world have at
least one cherished childhood memory involving

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:12.280
a Walt Disney film or character—the appeal
is simply universal, and somewhere in the

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.050
massive catalogue of Disney branded works
there’s a story for you as well.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.930
One that can inspire you, warm your heart,
make you laugh, or just remind you not to

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.760
forget to wish upon a star once in a while.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.920
But as for the legendary man behind the entertainment
powerhouse, there’s plenty more to him than

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.070
meets the eye.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:37.100
Here are some surprising facts you may not
already know about Walt Disney and the global

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:43.410
brand that he created.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.329
Walt Disney was born in 1901 as the fourth
of five children in a Chicago home built by

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:50.680
his father’s own two hands.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.440
It was this same self-made attitude which
would carry Walt through all of the successes

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.200
and setbacks during his life.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.200
According to his family, Walt’s favorite
meal was two cans of Hormel and Gebhardt’s

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.200
chili mixed together, with lemon Jell-O for
dessert.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.920
Despite his creative genius, Walt Disney “never
had any knack for business” according to

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.000
his brother Roy—a fact that would return
to haunt him throughout his career.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.820
Walt Disney Animation Studios wasn’t actually
Disney’s first business venture.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.450
In 1919, after a brief stint in France with
the Red Cross, Walt moved to Kansas City,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.880
Missouri—where he developed his passion
for art by taking a job as a newspaper artist.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.590
Within the next two years he began investigating
cel animation and founded an animation studio,

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:33.159
where he produced the Laugh-O-Grams series
of cartoons.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:38.760
By 1923, the studio was forced to declare
bankruptcy, proving that failure is just another

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.090
twist on the path to success.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.439
Perseverance is key, my friends.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:47.990
Disney was also a trend-setter in the realm
of television, creating popular children’s

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:52.880
programming like Zorro and Davy Crockett as
well as The Mickey Mouse Club, which spawned

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.330
the Mouseketeers and saw a modern revival
in the early nineties.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:02.750
Ryan Gosling, Britney Spears, and Justin Timberlake
are all former members of the Mouseketeers,

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:07.360
just to name a few contemporary celebrities
who had they early careers propelled by Disney

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.360
and the House of Mouse.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.730
Walt Disney wasn’t afraid to get involved
with political issues, stirring controversy

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:18.150
as one of the founders of the anti-communist
group Motion Picture Alliance for the Preservation

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.690
of American Ideals.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:25.849
He also produced many pieces of animated propaganda
during World War II—ranging from anti-Hitler

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:31.500
pieces to encouraging the timely payment of
taxes in order to bolster the war effort.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.560
However, Disney firmly stated that outside
of very specific instances during wartime

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:42.490
he and his team “work hard to see that nothing
of that sort creeps in”.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.940
The quality control folks at Disney can be
ruthless.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:51.290
“Part of Your World” was almost cut from
The Little Mermaid and “Can You Feel the

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:55.930
Love Tonight” was almost removed from The
Lion King.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:03.220
Beyoncé refused to audition for the role
of Princess Tiana and simply expected to be

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.260
offered the role.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.319
She lost the role to her “Dreamgirls”
co-star Anika Noni Rose.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:13.540
The people who voiced Minnie and Mickey Mouse
were married in real life.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.730
When Walt Disney was given an Oscar for Snow
White and the Seven Dwarfs, it was presented

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:23.379
to him as one normal-sized statuette and seven
miniature Oscars.

00:03:23.379 --> 00:03:28.340
Before Disney created the famous mouse mascot,
he had Oswald the Lucky Rabbit.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:35.360
But for Walt, Oswald was anything but lucky—after
being hired by Universal in 1927 to develop

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.540
the character, and using Oswald for two years
in 26 different cartoons, Disney discovered

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.400
that he didn’t actually retain any rights
to the character as an asset for use in his

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.780
own works.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:53.420
Ownership of Oswald was completely usurped
by another party shortly thereafter, so Walt

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:59.450
proceeded to conceive a new character which
would be wholly his—a happy-go-lucky, steamboat

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:02.770
piloting mouse by the name of Mickey.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:07.650
Almost eighty years later, Oswald rejoined
his younger brother under the Walt Disney

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.790
Animation Studios banner.

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:14.980
A tireless worker, Walt Disney was the creative
force driving the teams behind more than six

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:19.079
hundred cartoons, both feature length and
short form.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:24.479
As ranked by Forbes, the Disney brand is the
11th most valuable brand in the world as of

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:25.479
2015.

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:29.990
It is also the most valuable brand in the
leisure industry.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:34.980
Disneyland was a passion project for Walt’s
namesake, who dreamed of an idyllic place

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:40.169
for wholesome family fun—where children
could be themselves and adults could be kids

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:41.169
again.

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:45.879
It wasn’t to be just another amusement park,
but a celebration of mid-20th century America

00:04:45.879 --> 00:04:49.369
and a magical place where dreams could come
true.

00:04:49.369 --> 00:04:53.210
It opened to the public on July 17th, 1955.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:59.189
The site where the park was built in Anaheim,
California, was formerly an orange grove.

00:04:59.189 --> 00:05:03.029
Nestled in the heart of Disneyland’s New
Orleans Square is a secretive club Disney

00:05:03.029 --> 00:05:09.619
created for VIPs and discerning visitors to
the park, called Club 33, inspired by the

00:05:09.619 --> 00:05:14.409
private VIP lounges he visited at the 1964
World’s Fair.

00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:19.909
Becoming the member of the club is an appropriately
exclusive process, requiring a written letter

00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:25.639
of inquiry which places you on a wait list
for an opening, but promises world-class dining

00:05:25.639 --> 00:05:30.499
and a place of refuge for park visitors who
grow wearied of the traffic outside.

00:05:30.499 --> 00:05:36.020
Near the front of the park, on the second
floor of the Main Street Firehouse, Walt arranged

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.629
for the construction of a special apartment
for himself and his family to stay.

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:44.869
To this day, a light remains lit in the window
as a tribute to him.

00:05:44.869 --> 00:05:49.719
There are now Disneyland parks operating in
Tokyo, Paris, Hong Kong, and Shanghai—bringing

00:05:49.719 --> 00:05:53.559
Walt’s vision to the rest of the world.

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:58.729
This also means the sun is always shining
on at least one Disney park.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:03.569
Oh and there is a small basketball court inside
the Matterhorn at Disneyland, California.

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:06.499
But there’s also a dark side to Disneyland.

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:09.949
Numerous people have died at Disney parks.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:17.849
In 1966 a teenager was killed whilst attempting
to sneak into Disneyland by climbing onto

00:06:17.849 --> 00:06:19.580
the monorail track.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:24.189
He was struck by the train and dragged 12
metres down the track, despite a security

00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:27.180
guard yelling at him the whole time to stop.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.699
In fact, staff at the Haunted Mansion rides
at Disney parks have to constantly be on the

00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:34.159
lookout for people dumping ashes.

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:39.129
Strangely, having one’s ashes scattered
on the ride is a popular request, obviously

00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:41.610
Disney aren’t overly pleased about the trend.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:48.300
In 1970 yippies took over Tom Sawyer island
at Disneyland to stage a smoke-in to protest

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.929
Disneyland’s dress code.

00:06:49.929 --> 00:06:51.469
Disney was not amused.

00:06:51.469 --> 00:06:55.259
They closed the park early and called in the
riot police.

00:06:55.259 --> 00:07:01.529
Ever the innovator, in 1937 Walt Disney pioneered
the use of a new type of camera for filming

00:07:01.529 --> 00:07:05.469
animation—one which allowed for greater
depth to reinforce the illusion of the action

00:07:05.469 --> 00:07:08.599
on-screen occurring in three dimensions.

00:07:08.599 --> 00:07:13.509
The result was the “multiplane camera”,
first tested in one of Disney’s Silly Symphony

00:07:13.509 --> 00:07:18.469
shorts before being used in the filming of
Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.

00:07:18.469 --> 00:07:23.349
The success of Snow White spawned what would
be remembered as the “Golden Age of Animation”,

00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:28.960
as Walt Disney gained the financial stability
and public confidence necessary to produce

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.819
more films destined to become classics like
Pinocchio and Fantasia.

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:38.199
The California Institute of the Arts was the
brainchild of Walt Disney, who dreamed of

00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:42.279
a school for the performing and visual arts
where fellow artists could learn from and

00:07:42.279 --> 00:07:43.410
encourage one another.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:49.809
The staggering list of noteworthy CalArts
alumni includes animation legends Arlene Klasky,

00:07:49.809 --> 00:07:56.590
Tim Burton, Lauren Faust, and one whom many
consider to be Walt Disney’s spiritual successor:

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:02.819
John Lasseter, the acting chief creative officer
of Pixar and Walt Disney Animation Studios.

00:08:02.819 --> 00:08:08.159
To date, Walt Disney has received the most
Oscars out of any multiple Oscar recipient,

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:11.949
holding twenty-two of the prestigious awards
for his creative work.

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:17.379
He also holds the record for most Academy
Award nominations, with fifty-nine nominations

00:08:17.379 --> 00:08:18.789
to his credit.

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:22.659
His last Academy Award was granted posthumously.

00:08:22.659 --> 00:08:28.110
Seven of Walt Disney’s films have been deemed
so culturally significant to the United States

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:32.669
that they were selected for preservation by
the National Film Preservation Board of the

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:34.229
Library of Congress.

00:08:34.229 --> 00:08:38.690
Among their number are Mary Poppins, Steamboat
Willie, and Bambi.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:44.650
Decades of smoking eventually caught up with
Walt on November 11th of 1966, when doctors

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.879
discovered a walnut sized lump on his left
lung.

00:08:47.879 --> 00:08:52.670
By the time it was discovered the tumor had
metastasized, prompting the lung’s removal.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:59.839
He died on December 15th, 1966 of acute circulatory
collapse caused by the lung cancer.

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:05.850
Rumors persist that Disney wasn’t actually
cremated as claimed by his family, but instead

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:11.120
had his body cryogenically frozen and tucked
away for safekeeping beneath the Pirates of

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:17.810
the Caribbean ride at Disneyland, California—presumably
to be resuscitated at a later date, once medical

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:20.040
technology progressed sufficiently.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:27.740
Perhaps Tomorrowland was simply a conceptual
testing ground for living in the future?

